1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,920
A fluid fills all of
space and starts at rest.

2
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:09,520
A smooth force begins to stir it.
A thin vortex forms and tightens.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,200
Its peak speed grows without
bound as a finite time approaches,

4
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:16,850
while its total energy stays bounded.

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:19,780
On the eighth of September
twenty twenty-six,

6
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:24,300
OpenAI announced this construction
for the Navier–Stokes equations,

7
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:26,320
for every positive viscosity.

8
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:31,280
It addresses the two breakdown
alternatives of the Clay problem, C and D,

9
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:34,230
not the smooth-forever
alternatives A and B.

10
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:37,880
The proof is announced,
not yet independently refereed.

11
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,840
Its final statements are
also written in Lean.

12
00:00:44,117 --> 00:00:49,737
At each point x and time t,
the fluid has a velocity u, an arrow,

13
00:00:49,997 --> 00:00:53,877
and a pressure p, a number.
Follow one small parcel:

14
00:00:54,577 --> 00:00:58,247
its acceleration is the change
of u along its own path.

15
00:00:58,937 --> 00:01:03,617
The Navier–Stokes equations balance that
acceleration against the push of pressure,

16
00:01:04,397 --> 00:01:07,797
viscous friction of strength nu,
and an external force f.

17
00:01:08,497 --> 00:01:11,477
A second equation makes
the flow incompressible:

18
00:01:11,477 --> 00:01:15,047
as much fluid leaves each
tiny volume as enters it.

19
00:01:15,987 --> 00:01:20,397
Viscosity tends to smooth out differences
in velocity, the way heat spreads.

20
00:01:21,577 --> 00:01:25,047
Transport, the velocity carrying
itself along, is nonlinear.

21
00:01:25,847 --> 00:01:30,937
Smooth means that every derivative, in
space and time, exists and is continuous.

22
00:01:31,617 --> 00:01:34,787
Can smooth motion lose its
smoothness in a finite time?

23
00:01:37,850 --> 00:01:42,590
Charles Fefferman's official statement
poses the problem in all of space,

24
00:01:43,130 --> 00:01:46,470
and on the torus, a cube whose
opposite faces are identified.

25
00:01:47,370 --> 00:01:51,400
It has four alternatives,
each posed for every positive viscosity.

26
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:56,570
A and B ask for a smooth solution
for all time from every smooth,

27
00:01:56,650 --> 00:02:00,810
divergence-free initial velocity,
with no force. In all of space,

28
00:02:00,910 --> 00:02:04,600
the data must decay rapidly and
the energy must stay bounded.

29
00:02:05,450 --> 00:02:10,190
C and D ask for the opposite: admissible
initial data and a smooth force,

30
00:02:10,830 --> 00:02:14,150
chosen together, for which no
global smooth solution exists.

31
00:02:14,950 --> 00:02:18,580
The force must stay smooth and decaying
through the breakdown and after it.

32
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:22,110
A proof of any one
alternative is accepted.

33
00:02:22,630 --> 00:02:27,680
Breakdown with a force does not
settle A or B. Without a force,

34
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:30,700
smooth solutions might still last forever.

35
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:37,910
Without a force, the energy,
half the integral of the squared speed,

36
00:02:38,310 --> 00:02:43,760
can only decrease. But energy is a total.
It does not bound the peak speed.

37
00:02:44,330 --> 00:02:48,310
In nineteen thirty-four, Jean Leray
constructed global weak solutions;

38
00:02:49,230 --> 00:02:51,210
Eberhard Hopf later extended the theory.

39
00:02:51,930 --> 00:02:56,540
They satisfy the equations only in an
averaged sense, and need not be smooth.

40
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:01,940
Without force, Fujita and Kato proved
global smooth solutions for data small in

41
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:05,660
a critical norm,
relative to the viscosity. Caffarelli,

42
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:10,240
Kohn and Nirenberg proved that the
possible singular set is very small,

43
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:12,530
though a single point is not excluded.

44
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:16,500
Continuation criteria name a
quantity that must blow up:

45
00:03:17,140 --> 00:03:20,720
a vorticity integral for Euler,
by Beale, Kato and Majda,

46
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:26,100
and the L three norm for Navier–Stokes,
by Escauriaza, Seregin and Šverák,

47
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:27,620
both without force.

48
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:32,160
Terence Tao proved blowup for an averaged
equation that keeps the energy identity.

49
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:36,260
Jiajie Chen and Thomas Hou proved,
with computer assistance,

50
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:39,900
blowup for Euler without viscosity,
at a cylinder's wall.

51
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:42,710
Neither is a Navier–Stokes counterexample.

52
00:03:45,783 --> 00:03:50,003
Rescale a solution by a factor lambda:
space shrinks by lambda,

53
00:03:50,703 --> 00:03:54,343
time by lambda squared,
and the velocity grows by lambda.

54
00:03:54,883 --> 00:03:59,913
It still solves the equations, with the
same viscosity and a rescaled force.

55
00:04:00,443 --> 00:04:04,463
The L three norm does not change,
but the energy is divided by lambda.

56
00:04:05,503 --> 00:04:09,083
Packing a flow into ever smaller
regions costs ever less energy:

57
00:04:09,663 --> 00:04:12,763
in three dimensions,
the energy bound is supercritical.

58
00:04:13,003 --> 00:04:16,843
Put the blowup time at one,
and let tau be the time left.

59
00:04:17,543 --> 00:04:21,583
The announced core's radius
shrinks like tau to the one half.

60
00:04:22,303 --> 00:04:25,983
Its length shrinks like tau
to the one half minus h,

61
00:04:26,623 --> 00:04:28,383
for a small fixed number h.

62
00:04:29,263 --> 00:04:32,913
Its speed grows like tau to
the minus one half minus h.

63
00:04:33,873 --> 00:04:36,343
Multiply the squared speed
by the core's volume.

64
00:04:37,063 --> 00:04:40,923
The core's energy then scales like
tau to the one half minus three h,

65
00:04:41,343 --> 00:04:45,403
which tends to zero.
So the peak speed grows without bound,

66
00:04:45,983 --> 00:04:47,573
while the core's energy shrinks.

67
00:04:50,650 --> 00:04:55,290
A forced construction can start from the
answer. Choose a velocity and a pressure,

68
00:04:55,930 --> 00:04:59,190
put them into the equations,
and call what is left over the force.

69
00:04:59,610 --> 00:05:01,860
The equations then hold by definition.

70
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:06,130
The difficulty is the blowup time.
If the velocity blows up,

71
00:05:06,370 --> 00:05:10,090
the leftover usually does too,
and such a force is not allowed.

72
00:05:10,570 --> 00:05:12,950
The force must stay
smooth through time one.

73
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:17,310
Smooth is not the same as analytic.
This function is smooth,

74
00:05:17,310 --> 00:05:21,690
and every derivative vanishes at zero,
yet it is not zero.

75
00:05:22,230 --> 00:05:25,160
The official problem
asks only for smoothness.

76
00:05:28,217 --> 00:05:33,177
The construction has three layers.
First, a vortex core spirals inward,

77
00:05:33,937 --> 00:05:37,847
spins faster and leaves along its axis,
growing more slender as it shrinks.

78
00:05:38,487 --> 00:05:42,037
Joined to the flow outside,
it leaves an error in a ring around it:

79
00:05:42,337 --> 00:05:43,337
a missing stress.

80
00:05:43,617 --> 00:05:45,317
Oscillations can supply stress.

81
00:05:45,797 --> 00:05:49,717
A wave whose velocity averages to
zero still carries a flux of momentum,

82
00:05:49,717 --> 00:05:52,357
because the equations see
the velocity times itself,

83
00:05:52,797 --> 00:05:54,997
and a square averages
to something positive.

84
00:05:55,757 --> 00:05:57,877
But only positive
combinations are available.

85
00:05:58,657 --> 00:06:02,777
In a toy model with two directions, the
stresses that waves can make fill a cone,

86
00:06:03,057 --> 00:06:05,197
and a target outside it cannot be made.

87
00:06:06,157 --> 00:06:10,657
So the manuscript adjusts the core until
its error lies inside the actual cone.

88
00:06:11,497 --> 00:06:15,557
Localized pulses then deliver it:
the core's shear amplifies them,

89
00:06:15,997 --> 00:06:17,197
and viscosity damps them.

90
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:24,850
One repair is not enough.
Each correction creates new errors,

91
00:06:25,430 --> 00:06:27,770
and the force must be
smooth to every order.

92
00:06:28,710 --> 00:06:31,370
The manuscript cycles
through four corrections,

93
00:06:32,270 --> 00:06:33,810
rebuilding the pressure after each.

94
00:06:34,670 --> 00:06:37,910
Each full cycle raises the
order of the remaining error.

95
00:06:39,010 --> 00:06:42,010
For a fixed number of derivatives,
the losses stay fixed,

96
00:06:42,510 --> 00:06:46,950
so the order eventually passes them.
The ladder has no last rung.

97
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:51,530
Smooth cutoffs assemble the
corrections into one smooth field,

98
00:06:52,390 --> 00:06:55,430
and the force is extended
smoothly through time one,

99
00:06:55,770 --> 00:06:57,920
with compact support in space and time.

100
00:07:00,983 --> 00:07:06,483
Now the whole construction: the force
stays smooth through time one and after,

101
00:07:07,203 --> 00:07:10,623
the velocity becomes unbounded
as time approaches one,

102
00:07:11,123 --> 00:07:12,893
and the energy stays bounded.

103
00:07:13,673 --> 00:07:18,583
One step remains: could another smooth
solution, with the same force and start,

104
00:07:19,223 --> 00:07:22,983
last forever? The manuscript's
comparison argument says no,

105
00:07:23,563 --> 00:07:26,323
without assuming decay of
its derivatives or pressure.

106
00:07:26,853 --> 00:07:28,043
The end is elementary.

107
00:07:29,023 --> 00:07:32,423
Such a solution would be bounded
on a compact region up to time one,

108
00:07:32,923 --> 00:07:37,183
yet equal to a branch whose speed is
unbounded there. Given the construction,

109
00:07:37,763 --> 00:07:39,243
that contradiction gives C.

110
00:07:39,993 --> 00:07:44,143
For D, shrink the construction into
one cell of the torus and repeat it

111
00:07:44,143 --> 00:07:46,683
periodically. The copies do not interact,

112
00:07:47,063 --> 00:07:49,813
and a periodic comparison
finishes the argument.

113
00:07:52,883 --> 00:07:58,283
OpenAI's Lean development ends in two
theorems, for C and for D. Each says:

114
00:07:58,683 --> 00:08:00,023
for every positive viscosity,

115
00:08:00,563 --> 00:08:05,083
there are admissible initial data and
an admissible force for which no global

116
00:08:05,083 --> 00:08:06,533
smooth solution exists.

117
00:08:07,243 --> 00:08:11,583
Its predicates spell out the official
clauses: smoothness, incompressibility,

118
00:08:11,983 --> 00:08:15,903
the same initial data, decay,
finite energy with one uniform bound,

119
00:08:16,503 --> 00:08:20,943
periodic velocity and pressure. Compared
with Fefferman's text clause by clause,

120
00:08:21,363 --> 00:08:22,373
we found none missing.

121
00:08:22,783 --> 00:08:25,603
No hypothesis asks for
a vortex or an estimate.

122
00:08:26,443 --> 00:08:30,703
OpenAI's own record lists both theorems
with only the three standard axioms,

123
00:08:31,123 --> 00:08:35,283
and calls its review self-assessed.
Nothing was rerun for this film.

124
00:08:35,853 --> 00:08:38,233
In October, Alexander Bastounis,

125
00:08:38,833 --> 00:08:43,113
Fabian Circelli and Anders Hansen showed
two places where the Lean proof uses a

126
00:08:43,113 --> 00:08:45,613
different estimate from the paper:
one more derivative,

127
00:08:45,653 --> 00:08:49,573
and another pressure bound.
That refutes neither C nor D,

128
00:08:49,933 --> 00:08:52,423
but not every printed estimate
was checked as written.

129
00:08:55,483 --> 00:08:59,063
Mathematicians have begun to engage,
in preprints by Constantin,

130
00:08:59,443 --> 00:09:02,863
Ignatova and Vicol,
and by Zhen Lei and Xiao Ren.

131
00:09:03,543 --> 00:09:06,543
Clay still lists the problem as active,
MathDB as open,

132
00:09:06,903 --> 00:09:10,953
and the sources examined contain no
completed independent referee report.

133
00:09:11,993 --> 00:09:16,163
Nothing here sets the force to zero.
Alternatives A and B,

134
00:09:16,863 --> 00:09:21,443
smoothness for all time without force,
from all admissible data, remain open.

